@page "/docs/installation"
<PageTitle>Installation - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Installation</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">.NET 10</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">two paths</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p>Blaizio comes in two layers: <code>Blaizio.Base</code>, a NuGet package of headless primitives with behavior, accessibility and a <code>data-state</code> contract but no CSS, and a styled layer of Tailwind components that the <a href="docs/cli">Blaizio CLI</a> copies into your project as source you own. Most apps should take the CLI path, which sets up the full styled layer for you. Choose Base only when you are building your own design system on the primitives.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Prerequisites</h2>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
                <BzTableHeader>
                    <BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50">
                        <BzTableHead>Requirement</BzTableHead>
                        <BzTableHead>Notes</BzTableHead>
                    </BzTableRow>
                </BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>.NET 10 SDK</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Blaizio targets <code>net10.0</code>. Any Blazor flavor works: Server, WebAssembly, or Web App.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Tailwind CSS v4</BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The styled layer is Tailwind utilities. The CLI wires a compile pipeline for you, including a <em>standalone</em> mode that needs no Node at all.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                    <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap">Node.js <span class="text-muted-foreground">(optional)</span></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Needed only if you choose the <code>node</code> / <code>vite</code> / <code>postcss</code> Tailwind pipelines. The default <code>standalone</code> pipeline downloads a native binary instead.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                </BzTableBody>
            </BzTable>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Path 1: the CLI (recommended)</h2>
        <p>The CLI is a .NET global tool, so you install it once per machine:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Installation/InstallTool" />

        <h3 class="pt-2">Start a new app</h3>
        <p>When you are starting from an empty folder, <code>blaizio new showcase</code> scaffolds a complete Blazor WebAssembly app in the current folder:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>the csproj, referencing <code>Blaizio.Base</code>, <code>Blaizio.Icons</code>, <code>Blaizio.Icons.Tabler</code> and <code>TailwindMerge.NET</code>;</li>
            <li>a layout with a sidebar, command palette and dark mode toggle, plus demo pages;</li>
            <li><code>index.html</code> with the pre-paint <code>boot.js</code> script already wired;</li>
            <li>the tokens file, which holds the Tailwind input and your theme values;</li>
            <li>a curated set of about 35 components in <code>Components/Ui</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The app compiles and runs straight away:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Installation/InitNew" />

        <h3 class="pt-2">Add Blaizio to an existing app</h3>
        <p>For a project you already have, run <code>add</code> in the project folder. The first run wires Blaizio into the project and then copies the components you named. It only edits files that already exist and never scaffolds new pages:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Installation/InitExisting" />
        <p>The wiring runs these steps in order:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Writes <code>blaizio.json</code> with the namespace, output folder, style and registry. The <a href="docs/cli">CLI page</a> documents the schema.</li>
            <li>Runs <code>dotnet add package</code> for <code>Blaizio.Base</code>, <code>Blaizio.Icons</code>, <code>Blaizio.Icons.Tabler</code> and <code>TailwindMerge.NET</code>.</li>
            <li>Creates the tokens file <code>Styles/app.css</code>, one file you own that holds the Tailwind input and every theme value. An existing file is topped up, never overwritten. It also git-ignores <code>.blaizio/</code>, because the <code>Blaizio.Base</code> package regenerates the static contract sheets there on every <code>dotnet build</code>, much like <code>obj/</code>.</li>
            <li>Sets up the Tailwind pipeline so that <code>dotnet build</code> compiles <code>wwwroot/app.css</code>.</li>
            <li>Patches your existing host page in place, inserting the stylesheet link and the pre-paint <code>boot.js</code> script. That page is <code>wwwroot/index.html</code> for WebAssembly, <code>Components/App.razor</code> for a Blazor Web App, or <code>Pages/_Host.cshtml</code> / <code>_Layout.cshtml</code> for Blazor Server.</li>
            <li>Adds any components you listed.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Every copied file is rewritten to <em>your</em> namespace and recorded in <code>blaizio.json</code>, which is what lets <code>add --diff</code> and <code>blaizio update</code> work on it later. Running <code>add</code> or <code>update</code> again restores anything missing without disturbing what is already there.</p>

        <h3 class="pt-2">Wire up the app</h3>
        <p>Two edits are still yours to make, unless you started from the showcase template, which already has both. First, add the single <code>AddBlaizio</code> call to <code>Program.cs</code>. It registers every service Blaizio needs, and its options lambda is where app-wide component defaults go:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Installation/Register" />
        <p>Second, if you use the imperative <a href="docs/dialog-service">dialog</a> or <a href="docs/components/toast">toast</a> services, render their provider components once at the app root, for example in <code>MainLayout.razor</code>:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Installation/Providers" />
        <p>You do not need to edit the host page by hand. The wiring patches whichever host your app has (<code>wwwroot/index.html</code>, <code>Components/App.razor</code>, or <code>Pages/_Host.cshtml</code> / <code>_Layout.cshtml</code>), and afterwards it contains the stylesheet link and the <code>boot.js</code> script shown here:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Installation/IndexHtml" />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Path 2: Base only (headless)</h2>
        <p>If you are building your own design system, <code>Blaizio.Base</code> stands alone. Every primitive renders the correct element with the correct ARIA roles, keyboard behavior and a <code>data-state</code> attribute contract, and ships no CSS at all. You style the states (<code>data-state="open"</code>, <code>data-[side=top]</code>, ...) with whatever you prefer: Tailwind, plain CSS or CSS modules.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Installation/BaseOnly" />
        <p>Register the services with the same <code>AddBlaizio()</code> call in <code>Program.cs</code> shown above. The stores, the imperative services (<code>IDialogService</code>, <code>IToastService</code>), <code>IThemeService</code> and <code>BlaizioOptions</code> all live in Base, so nothing else needs registering. Compared with the CLI path:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>You compose <code>Base*</code> primitives (<code>BaseDialog</code>, <code>BaseTooltip</code>, <code>BaseCalendar</code>, ...) instead of the styled <code>Bz*</code> components, and supply your own classes via each primitive's <code>Class</code> parameter.</li>
            <li>For the imperative APIs, you host <code>BaseDialogProvider</code> / <code>BaseToastProvider</code> yourself and render their content templates with your own styling.</li>
            <li>The theme and style machinery still works. <code>boot.js</code>, <code>IThemeService</code> and the theme switcher primitives only toggle classes and the <code>dir</code> attribute on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, and your stylesheets decide what those classes mean.</li>
            <li>There is no <code>blaizio.json</code>, no registry and no Tailwind requirement, because Base is a plain NuGet package.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>You can also mix the two. Start with Base only, then run <code>blaizio add</code> later to pull in styled components for the parts you would rather not build by hand.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Verify it works</h2>
        <p>To confirm a CLI setup, work through these checks:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Run <code>dotnet build</code>. It should compile <code>wwwroot/app.css</code>, so confirm the file exists and is not empty. The same build writes the contract sheets into <code>.blaizio/</code>.</li>
            <li>Run <code>blaizio info</code> to print the resolved configuration.</li>
            <li>Put <code>&lt;BzButton&gt;Hello&lt;/BzButton&gt;</code> on a page. If it renders styled, the whole chain (packages, copied source, Tailwind and the tokens file) is working.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Whenever you change the theme colors later, run <code>blaizio contrast</code> to check that they still meet WCAG AA.</p>
    </section>

